            <ArticleTitle>[Anxiety and depression in patients with medical diseases].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>354-67</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Anxiety and depression are more prevalent in patients with medical diseases than in the general population. The current diagnostic systems for depression/anxiety in the field of comorbidity, however, are problematic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS AND CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Higher comorbidity has deteriorating consequences in all bio-psycho-social respects. The aetiologic connections between medical disease and psychological disorders are complex bidirectional somato-psycho-somatic ones. Seven modalities of comorbidity for coronary heart disease (myocardial infarction) and depression/anxiety are discussed. The psychopharmacological and psychotherapeutic treatment of anxiety/depression in patients with medical diseases follows general guidelines.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
